AB  - To efficiently predict dynamic fracture evolution in Multiphase Composite Structures (MCS), a Spatiotemporal collaborative Encoder–Decoder deep learning framework (ST-EnDec) driven by Finite Particle Method (FPM) data is proposed. Taking concrete as an example, crack evolution data across multiple loading rates are first generated automatically via a Graphics Processing Units (GPU)-accelerated FPM. Subsequently, a multidimensional differentiated normalized feature system integrating geometry, material, phase, and rate effects is designed to preserve physical interpretability. Furthermore, a multi-head Graph Attention Network (GAT) encoder is coupled with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) decoder to capture spatiotemporal coupling. Finally, a composite loss function weighted by dynamic masked mean squared error, binary cross-entropy, and Wasserstein distance is established to enhance the sensitivity to high-damage regions. The results demonstrate that on untrained loading rates, R2=0.85, MAE = 0.04, RMSE = 0.12 are achieved for damage prediction, with 96.4 % fracture classification accuracy; rate-dependent fracture patterns are reproduced. Single-case inference requires only 20 ms, three orders of magnitude faster than GPU-FPM simulation. The method is physically consistent and computationally efficient, enabling effective fracture prediction for MCS.
