AB  - Gradient compression is a crucial technology for mitigating communication bottlenecks in distributed deep learning (DDL). However, achieving significant performance improvements with gradient compression remains challenging. In practical applications, gradient compression faces several challenges: (1) it fails to effectively optimize the initial overhead of small-scale tensor communication; (2) compression operations might compete with tensor computations for GPU resources, thereby delaying gradient transmission; (3) it can potentially cause accuracy issues that require careful handling. To maximize the benefits of gradient compression and address these challenges, we propose the Cocktails system. Additionally, to circumvent contention for GPU compute and memory resources, Cocktails offloads compression operations to the CPU and employs a model backup technique that decouples read dependencies on model parameters across worker nodes, thus hiding CPU compression costs and partial communication overhead. Cocktails optimizes and enhances the performance of existing 1-bit and 2-bit compression algorithms through its innovative strategies. Experimental evaluations on a 16-V100 GPU cluster demonstrate that for typical DDL training tasks, the Cocktails system improves the training throughput of the 1-bit and 2-bit compression algorithms by 74.3% and 68.7%, respectively. Furthermore, it enhances convergence accuracy by 1.1% to 2.3% and reduces GPU memory consumption by 10.3%. Notably, with the 2-bit compression algorithm, Cocktails matches the accuracy of lossless precision training and outperforms current state-of-the-art methods by up to 56% in training throughput.
