AB  - Deep-learning workloads on graphics processing units (GPUs) consume substantial electrical energy and therefore contribute non-negligibly to the environmental footprint of modern artificial intelligence systems. Existing tools for energy and carbon accounting typically operate at coarse temporal resolution, focus on central processing unit (CPU)-centric measurements, or require low-level access that is not available on mainstream cloud platforms. This article presents GpuEnergyUsageCallback, a Python software tool that enables batch-level monitoring of GPU energy consumption during deep-learning training in TensorFlow/Keras. The software builds on the NVIDIA Management Library (NVML) and its actively maintained Python bindings, providing a reusable callback that records per-batch telemetry and converts it into kilowatt-hour estimates using standard numerical integration. This work contributes mainly to tools and system integration, not a new energy estimation method. The software allows fine-grained measurement of GPU energy within restricted, managed cloud services. The implementation is designed for managed cloud environments such as Kaggle and Google Colab, where users rely on high-level Python application programming interfaces (APIs) and lack administrative control over GPU hardware. To demonstrate the tool, a case study on the Cassava leaf-disease classification task compares EfficientNet, DenseNet, and ResNet architectures in terms of validation accuracy, training time, and GPU energy usage. The results illustrate how batch-level traces support model selection and Green AI education by revealing trade-offs between accuracy and energy. The software is released as open-source code with a worked example notebook to facilitate integration into existing workflows.
