KW  - Deadline-aware scheduler
AB  - Modern GPU clusters are widely used to train distributed deep learning (DL) jobs, where scheduling decisions directly affect both system efficiency and user experience. In practice, DL jobs often come with deadlines, and failing to meet them can have serious consequences for users. Designing effective schedulers for such environments is challenging: scheduling decisions are tightly coupled, the problem is NP-hard, and most existing approaches either optimize job selection or GPU allocation in isolation, while largely overlooking deadline awareness and preemption. In this paper, we introduce PriorityDRL, a deadline-aware deep reinforcement learning (DRL) scheduler, with one agent for job selection and another for GPU allocation, jointly optimizing scheduling decisions. The job selection agent prioritizes jobs based on deadline urgency and supports preemption, allowing the system to respond dynamically to urgent jobs. The GPU allocation agent assigns jobs to physically proximal GPUs, reducing communication costs and mitigating resource fragmentation. By coordinating these complementary decisions, PriorityDRL balances deadline satisfaction with overall scheduling efficiency. Extensive experiments on real-world GPU cluster traces show that PriorityDRL consistently reduces missed deadlines and deadline lateness, improves GPU utilization, and achieves competitive job completion times compared to state-of-the-art heuristic and DRL-based schedulers. These results demonstrate that explicitly accounting for deadlines while separating job selection from GPU allocation is crucial for efficient and reliable operation in modern, shared GPU clusters.
