AB  - In this study, the deep learning-based surrogate models are developed for the rapid and accurate prediction of transient concentration gradients in microfluidic mass transfer devices with various cavity configurations and channel heights. Time-dependent concentration datasets are established using numerical simulations in COMSOL Multiphysics. Two decoupled deep neural networks are established to separately predict time-dependent outlet concentration scalar and reconstruct steady two-dimensional cross-sectional concentration maps under fixed geometry and operating conditions. The proposed unified surrogate model achieves R2 > 0.99 for within-sample temporal prediction via blocked temporal split; under strict leave-one-geometry-out cross-validation on unseen geometric configurations, the average R2 reaches 0.89, and the target circular cavity design attains an R2 of 0.96. Remarkably, after offline training, the well-trained DNN achieves over 120,000 times faster inference speed for single-case prediction compared with a standalone COMSOL transient simulation, demonstrating the improvement in computational efficiency for microscale mass transfer analysis. The results confirm that the DNN model can precisely capture the transient mass transport characteristics and concentration dynamics within different microfluidic cavity structures. This work provides a data-driven tool for the fast design, performance optimization, and dynamic monitoring of microfluidic concentration gradient generators. The proposed framework can be further extended to accelerate multiphysics simulations, thermal-fluid analysis, and mass transfer studies in a broad range of microscale heat and mass transfer devices.
