KW  - Deadline and fairness aware scheduler
KW  - Mixed deep-learning training and inference workloads
KW  - Resource fragmentation utilization
AB  - The widespread development of Deep Learning (DL) applications has surged DL workloads across numerous fields. To accelerate DL workloads in Graph Process Unit (GPU) clusters, most researches target to optimally scheduling training or inference workloads alone. However, diverse demands by mixed workloads turn to resource preemption and fragmentation that resulting in cluster inefficiency. To tackle this issue, we proposed DeepPlus, an adaptive deadline- and fairness-aware scheduler for accelerating mixed training and inference workloads. To start with, we established a job-level model considering various Service-Level Objective (SLO) requirements and a resource-level model accounting for fairness to improve cluster efficiency. Subsequently, we designed a job classification and preemption estimation scheme (JCPS) to categorize jobs with various SLO demands and profile job preemption possibilities. To meet deadline constraint, we devised job deadline-aware scheduling scheme (JDAS) to dynamically schedule training jobs considering jobs’ resource usage and SLO demands. Furthermore, a fairness-aware resource fragment utilization scheme (FRUS) was implemented and leveraged to reduce the fragments from inference jobs’ allocation while accounting for fairness issue. Finally, a simulation was conducted using a Microsoft DL workload. The experimental results demonstrated that, compared with Tiresias, Themis, Pollux, and DeepBoot, DeepPlus can achieve an overall maximum improvement of 9.2 %, 25.9 % and 20.1 % makespan reduction across small, middle, and large workload scales while satisfying deadline and fairness constraints. Simultaneously, the effectiveness of DeepPlus was discussed in terms of fairness, larger-scale cluster, key parameter variation, and various job submission rates under diurnal periodicity.
