KW  - GPU acceleration
KW  - Finite difference
KW  - MODFLOW 6
KW  - NVIDIA warp
AB  - Repeated steady-state groundwater solves for calibration and uncertainty analysis are often limited by runtime. We present DarcyWarp, a graphics processing unit (GPU)-accelerated geometric multigrid solver for two-dimensional, steady-state, single-layer groundwater flow using a fixed-transmissivity formulation on structured grids. DarcyWarp implements an explicit cell-integrated five-point finite-difference operator and multigrid cycle as Python kernels just in time compiled for GPU execution with NVIDIA Warp, and supports areal source/sink terms, prescribed head, no-flow, and general-head boundaries. Validation against a sparse finite-difference direct solve and MODFLOW 6 on matched grids shows close head agreement, consistent residuals, and mass-balance closure. Benchmarks span matched-grid single-instance cases from 104 to 9×106 active cells and two 48-case repeated-solve ensembles. DarcyWarp reaches about 70 times end-to-end speedup over MODFLOW 6 in the single-instance suite and order-of-magnitude throughput gains in ensembles, enabling reproducible repeated-solve workflows on commodity GPUs.
